900
01:43:35,446 --> 01:43:39,026
We house the second-largest insect collection in the

901
01:43:39,106 --> 01:43:42,806
world, with an estimated 35 million specimens.

902
01:43:44,006 --> 01:43:47,506
Our collection represents over 400,000 species and

903
01:43:47,566 --> 01:43:51,186
houses over 130,000 primary type specimens.

904
01:43:51,606 --> 01:43:53,326
Please remember that last number.

905
01:43:54,246 --> 01:43:57,666
It will come up again, and this is all managed with a

906
01:43:57,726 --> 01:43:59,526
relatively very small staff.

907
01:44:00,926 --> 01:44:04,846
So today I'm talking to you about the difficulties

908
01:44:04,966 --> 01:44:08,726
of maintaining legacy data over platforms that have essentially

909
01:44:08,966 --> 01:44:12,886
aged themselves out of use, and our department's work

910
01:44:12,966 --> 01:44:16,766
to synchronize and modernize them into new databases.

911
01:44:17,666 --> 01:44:21,586
So on the screen, I have a page from our first version of a

912
01:44:21,626 --> 01:44:25,246
database. It's the original handwritten type ledger

913
01:44:25,806 --> 01:44:29,566
catalog books. So our department has been using these books

914
01:44:29,626 --> 01:44:33,606
since 1896, which means they have seen 130 years

915
01:44:33,806 --> 01:44:35,606
of continuous use by the department.

916
01:44:36,606 --> 01:44:38,406
The type numbers are assigned in the book.

917
01:44:38,646 --> 01:44:42,486
They're attached to the specimen, and then they were used in the publications.

918
01:44:43,146 --> 01:44:46,706
So these books recorded information that includes that original

919
01:44:46,766 --> 01:44:50,746
catalog number, the species name, the sex, the locality that it was

920
01:44:50,826 --> 01:44:54,746
found at, the year the specimen was collected, who sent it to

921
01:44:54,866 --> 01:44:58,806
us, who collected it, and then the date it was entered into the

922
01:44:58,846 --> 01:45:02,666
ledger. And again, we regularly used these books up

923
01:45:02,766 --> 01:45:04,326
to the 1990s.

924
01:45:05,186 --> 01:45:09,026
But by the last physical ledger, the department was no longer recording

925
01:45:09,106 --> 01:45:12,186
that last item when the data was being entered into them.

926
01:45:13,466 --> 01:45:17,386
And they stopped that because, in the 1990s, or rather the

927
01:45:17,426 --> 01:45:21,006
mid-late 1980s, our department built its first

928
01:45:21,166 --> 01:45:23,666
electronic type database called Inquire.

929
01:45:24,326 --> 01:45:28,066
It was built in-house by museum staff, and it captured

930
01:45:28,126 --> 01:45:31,546
data at the level you should expect from the 1980s.

931
01:45:32,726 --> 01:45:35,286
But then technology moves pretty fast.

932
01:45:36,946 --> 01:45:40,866
By the early 1990s, we were already building a replacement database

933
01:45:40,926 --> 01:45:41,286
system.

934
01:45:42,326 --> 01:45:46,306
The museum switched to something called the Collection Records Information

935
01:45:46,406 --> 01:45:49,366
System, or CRIS, in the mid-1990s.

936
01:45:50,166 --> 01:45:52,706
And that was another built in-house system.

937
01:45:53,706 --> 01:45:57,626
It also allowed for more relational database structure, so it could

938
01:45:57,686 --> 01:46:00,866
