3972
06:59:09,442 --> 06:59:12,662
make use of the part of material entity

3973
06:59:13,082 --> 06:59:16,702
relationship in Darwin Core and express in this way

3974
06:59:17,142 --> 06:59:20,442
something quite similar to what we express in DINA.

3975
06:59:21,682 --> 06:59:23,262
So apart from maybe some

3976
06:59:24,762 --> 06:59:27,982
semantic blurriness, this mapping also works quite well,

3977
06:59:28,762 --> 06:59:32,442
in this regard. However, there was one issue, and that is that in

3978
06:59:32,502 --> 06:59:36,142
DINA, we not only store that it's a mud shrimp organism with its

3979
06:59:36,182 --> 06:59:39,482
scientific name, we also store that it's an adult male mud shrimp.

3980
06:59:41,242 --> 06:59:42,602
If we want to

3981
06:59:43,562 --> 06:59:45,302
include this information here,

3982
06:59:46,442 --> 06:59:50,082
there is no room in the material concept in

3983
06:59:50,262 --> 06:59:53,022
Darwin Core. At the moment, this would require us to

3984
06:59:54,002 --> 06:59:57,302
add an additional table, like a table for occurrences,

3985
06:59:57,722 --> 06:59:58,302
only to

3986
06:59:59,782 --> 07:00:02,482
include also the information about life stage and sex

3987
07:00:03,402 --> 07:00:05,822
in there. However, I mentioned at the beginning,

3988
07:00:06,802 --> 07:00:10,082
certain concerns about really generating

3989
07:00:10,162 --> 07:00:13,982
occurrences when in our source data format, we don't

3990
07:00:14,022 --> 07:00:15,842
have occurrences. That's the case for DINA.

3991
07:00:16,502 --> 07:00:18,902
DINA does not have the occurrence concept,

3992
07:00:20,002 --> 07:00:23,522
and it creates downstream issues now to generate

3993
07:00:23,582 --> 07:00:27,262
occurrences just for the sake of exporting the data.

3994
07:00:28,302 --> 07:00:31,042
So this is actually an option we are not in favor of.

3995
07:00:31,822 --> 07:00:35,742
I don't know what could be for this very specific, detailed case, I'm

3996
07:00:35,782 --> 07:00:39,262
sorry. Other options, maybe just put sex and life stage

3997
07:00:39,302 --> 07:00:43,042
information also on the material, but that's not according to the

3998
07:00:43,082 --> 07:00:46,082
Darwin Core schema, at least as of now.

3999
07:00:46,462 --> 07:00:48,161
I don't know if this might change in the future.

4000
07:00:48,522 --> 07:00:51,362
Or a third option would be to use material assertions.

4001
07:00:52,122 --> 07:00:55,802
It looks quite elegant to put the information there, but it imposes

4002
07:00:55,902 --> 07:00:57,762
also the question whether then

4003
07:00:58,642 --> 07:01:02,462
later downstream data consumers of this, like GBIF or

4004
07:01:02,642 --> 07:01:06,562
finally the end users also find the data which they are looking

4005
07:01:06,622 --> 07:01:10,482
for if they are specifically interested in these attributes, which in other

4006
07:01:10,542 --> 07:01:13,802
places in the schema have a dedicated attribute.

4007
07:01:15,402 --> 07:01:19,102
So this again, also, I think a solution that is possible,

4008
07:01:20,142 --> 07:01:22,862
but it would also require, let's say

4009
07:01:22,962 --> 07:01:26,322
further

4010
07:01:26,382 --> 07:01:30,022
agreements between data publishers of this type of material

4011
07:01:30,082 --> 07:01:33,722
data without occurrences, and also agreement with

4012
07:01:33,782 --> 07:01:35,082
downstream data processes.

4013
07:01:36,042 --> 07:01:39,122
With this, I already come to the conclusions.

4014
07:01:40,542 --> 07:01:44,522
DINA's data model is already widely aligned with the new Darwin

4015
07:01:44,582 --> 07:01:48,322
Core conceptual model, specifically material entity

4016
07:01:48,382 --> 07:01:52,122
lineage and also material entities with complex

4017
07:01:52,182 --> 07:01:53,422
mixed organism material

4018
07:01:54,562 --> 07:01:58,242
can be represented. And coming back to this

4019
07:01:58,302 --> 07:02:00,302
pentagon of different Darwin Core

4020
07:02:02,042 --> 07:02:05,822
concepts and their relationships, what is here on the right side in the

4021
07:02:06,362 --> 07:02:10,222
green box, this is what really works well and what is aligned

4022
07:02:10,282 --> 07:02:10,922
with DINA's

4023
07:02:12,702 --> 07:02:15,242
collection management system internal data model.

4024
07:02:15,782 --> 07:02:19,362
What is outside of it, the occurrences, well, it's not aligned.

4025
07:02:19,422 --> 07:02:23,202
And this simply would mean for us as a data publisher that in the

4026
07:02:23,402 --> 07:02:27,222
future we aim to mobilize our data as Darwin

4027
07:02:27,282 --> 07:02:30,002
Core data packages, but without occurrences.

4028
07:02:31,002 --> 07:02:34,902
Really focus only on the classes that are represented in our

4029
07:02:35,502 --> 07:02:36,482
internal data model.

4030
07:02:38,066 --> 07:02:42,006
And then if downstream data consumers, it could be

4031
07:02:42,066 --> 07:02:44,286
GBIF, if they really require occurrences,

4032
07:02:45,286 --> 07:02:49,206
they could use still our data, and they can generate then the

4033
07:02:49,266 --> 07:02:53,126
occurrences based on the data according to their

4034
07:02:53,186 --> 07:02:56,926
internal requirements. But for this, we need, as I

4035
07:02:56,986 --> 07:03:00,806
mentioned, additional conventions between the

4036
07:03:00,866 --> 07:03:03,906
data publishers and data consumers for

4037
07:03:04,546 --> 07:03:06,646
sharing this type of material data.

4038
07:03:07,466 --> 07:03:07,886
Thank you.

4039
07:03:10,526 --> 07:03:11,606
Sorry. Acknowledgments.

4040
07:03:14,206 --> 07:03:15,166
Thanks very much, Jonas.

4041
07:03:16,826 --> 07:03:20,346
Our next talk is a virtual one, so it will be

4042
07:03:20,766 --> 07:03:21,326
a movie.

4043
07:03:22,486 --> 07:03:24,846
And that comes from Katie Pearson.

4044
07:03:25,726 --> 07:03:29,466
It's entitled "Sharing Extended Specimen Data from Symbiota Portals

4045
07:03:29,566 --> 07:03:31,166
Using Registered Extensions."

4046
07:03:35,526 --> 07:03:39,066
Hi, my name is Katie Pearson, and I'm here to talk about extended

4047
07:03:39,146 --> 07:03:43,006
specimen data in Symbiota Portals and how we're sharing them by following

4048
07:03:43,106 --> 07:03:43,546
standards.

4049
07:03:45,746 --> 07:03:48,826
Symbiota is an open-source biodiversity data management software.

4050
07:03:48,986 --> 07:03:52,446
It's kind of a mini aggregator as well as a collection management system.

4051
07:03:52,946 --> 07:03:55,766
It's open source, so it's freely available on GitHub.

4052
07:03:57,246 --> 07:04:00,086
And it underlies about 60 or more

4053
07:04:00,666 --> 07:04:04,466
biodiversity portals. About 52 of them are hosted

4054
07:04:04,546 --> 07:04:08,286
at the University of Kansas, at the Symbiota Support Hub, and

4055
07:04:08,346 --> 07:04:12,086
they are used widely by over 2,000 collections to

4056
07:04:12,146 --> 07:04:13,986
share data with the wide world.

4057
07:04:14,926 --> 07:04:18,746
This is just an example of the Mid-Atlantic Herbaria Consortium, a project of

4058
07:04:18,766 --> 07:04:22,166
herbaria across the Mid-Atlantic region of the United States, who all share their

4059
07:04:22,206 --> 07:04:23,306
data via this portal.

4060
07:04:25,146 --> 07:04:28,466
Symbiota Portals are places where communities of practice can work together.

4061
07:04:28,686 --> 07:04:32,226
Many collections are sharing data alongside one another, and they are

4062
07:04:32,266 --> 07:04:35,646
publicly searchable, and there are many tools for creating checklists, viewing

4063
07:04:35,726 --> 07:04:37,146
images, mapping, et cetera.

4064
07:04:38,406 --> 07:04:41,326
Here's an example of a map. These are interactive, and then there are

4065
07:04:41,686 --> 07:04:44,106
georeferencing tools for the collection managers as well.

4066
07:04:45,485 --> 07:04:49,466
As I mentioned, Symbiota Portals are also collection management systems, so they

4067
07:04:49,506 --> 07:04:53,446
can be used as web interfaces for a database.

4068
07:04:53,626 --> 07:04:57,046
So they can be your primary database where you do all your data entry, your

4069
07:04:57,126 --> 07:05:01,046
editing, and your management. This is an example of the data entry

4070
07:05:01,106 --> 07:05:04,746
page, and there are many tools for georeferencing, for

4071
07:05:05,486 --> 07:05:07,586
connecting to the taxonomic thesaurus, et cetera.

4072
07:05:10,106 --> 07:05:13,726
The key design of Symbiota is that it's low barrier to entry

4073
07:05:13,766 --> 07:05:17,546
mobilization of data. So we're trying to make things as easy to get into

4074
07:05:17,686 --> 07:05:21,286
as possible because we know that collections folks' time are

4075
07:05:21,326 --> 07:05:22,786
limited, and their ability to

4076
07:05:23,666 --> 07:05:27,346
learn new systems is limited by that lack of time.

4077
07:05:27,906 --> 07:05:28,326
So

4078
07:05:29,406 --> 07:05:32,846
while we try to keep everything aligned with Darwin Core data standards, we also

4079
07:05:32,886 --> 07:05:35,706
want to make sure that the data's really easy to import and export.

4080
07:05:36,226 --> 07:05:38,366
Sometimes aligning the standards makes that even easier.

4081
07:05:39,066 --> 07:05:42,586
And then there are built-in publishing tools so that the data can go directly to

4082
07:05:42,646 --> 07:05:44,926
GBIF instead of through an integrated publishing toolkit.

4083
07:05:45,226 --> 07:05:48,786
So we're really trying to make things as easy as possible, low barrier to entry for

4084
07:05:48,826 --> 07:05:49,346
the users.

4085
07:05:51,506 --> 07:05:54,986
So today, I'm going to be talking about how Symbiota stores and shares

4086
07:05:55,046 --> 07:05:57,646
data that's kind of considered extended data.

4087
07:05:57,706 --> 07:06:01,446
So not the primary who, what, where of the specimens, but the

4088
07:06:01,526 --> 07:06:05,226
data on top of that, such as annotations, such as media, material

4089
07:06:05,306 --> 07:06:06,846
sample, traits, things like that.

4090
07:06:07,386 --> 07:06:10,146
And this is, again, a picture of the data entry page, and

4091
07:06:11,166 --> 07:06:15,086
you can see these tabs along the top, and that is how we visualize the

4092
07:06:15,126 --> 07:06:17,886
extended data for the collection managers.

4093
07:06:17,926 --> 07:06:21,126
So they will be seeing these different tabs, and that's where they can manage the

4094
07:06:21,166 --> 07:06:21,926
extended data.

4095
07:06:22,766 --> 07:06:26,226
Some of the data extensions are included on the main entry page.

4096
07:06:26,266 --> 07:06:30,166
For example, additional identifiers can be seen in this kind of table

4097
07:06:30,206 --> 07:06:33,866
within the table here, but most of the extensions are viewable on these

4098
07:06:33,926 --> 07:06:34,286
tabs.

4099
07:06:36,646 --> 07:06:37,846
So this is the primary

4100
07:06:39,886 --> 07:06:43,266
data package or data view. Most of it is

4101
07:06:43,326 --> 07:06:46,606
occurrence-based. It's occurrence-based rather than event-based because

4102
07:06:46,666 --> 07:06:50,526
occurrences kind of have traditionally been along with the

4103
07:06:50,606 --> 07:06:51,446
idea of the specimen.

4104
07:06:52,866 --> 07:06:56,586
And we have occurrences as kind of the main core, and then all of the extensions

4105
07:06:56,606 --> 07:07:00,406
that I'm going to talk about today include kind of the old-fashioned multimedia and

4106
07:07:00,486 --> 07:07:04,386
identifications. But some of the newer implementations are

4107
07:07:04,466 --> 07:07:08,326
using extended measurement or fact, material sample, resource relationship, and the

4108
07:07:08,366 --> 07:07:10,186
alternative identifiers extension.

4109
07:07:10,526 --> 07:07:13,846
So we now support data from all of these extensions in Symbiota Portals.

4110
07:07:15,366 --> 07:07:19,006
So first I'll talk about the two general ones, are multimedia and

4111
07:07:19,066 --> 07:07:22,806
identifications. These are pretty self-explanatory. I go back to this page.

4112
07:07:23,166 --> 07:07:27,046
There is a tab for media. There's a tab for the determination history,

4113
07:07:27,106 --> 07:07:29,726
and this is the identification history of those specimens.

4114
07:07:30,346 --> 07:07:34,126
So these are pretty easy for folks to wrap their minds

4115
07:07:34,166 --> 07:07:37,866
around. They've been around for a very long time, and they will keep

4116
07:07:37,926 --> 07:07:38,666
track of

4117
07:07:39,626 --> 07:07:43,346
how many times that specimen has been identified and any of the multimedia

4118
07:07:44,326 --> 07:07:45,506
associated with those records.

4119
07:07:47,506 --> 07:07:50,486
Since those ones are somewhat easy to think about, I'm going to talk a little bit

4120
07:07:50,606 --> 07:07:52,686
longer about the other newer ones.

4121
07:07:53,266 --> 07:07:56,606
So after the multimedia and identifications, those have been around for a long

4122
07:07:56,666 --> 07:08:00,306
time. We implemented the extended measurement or fact extension,

4123
07:08:00,846 --> 07:08:03,426
and the reason for this is that we had a project

4124
07:08:04,366 --> 07:08:07,866
that was trying to capture phenological data from herbarium

4125
07:08:07,926 --> 07:08:11,626
specimens. And so we were trying to wrap our heads around how we might be able to

4126
07:08:11,686 --> 07:08:15,166
capture that data in a useful way. And really it came down to

4127
07:08:15,206 --> 07:08:18,086
the extended measurement or fact extension really

4128
07:08:18,826 --> 07:08:20,466
incorporated everything that we needed to.

4129
07:08:20,686 --> 07:08:23,946
We, in fact, looked into developing a new standard for phenology, and just

4130
07:08:24,026 --> 07:08:24,906
everything seemed to be

4131
07:08:26,546 --> 07:08:29,766
packageable according to the extended measurement or fact just fine.

4132
07:08:30,726 --> 07:08:30,906
So,

4133
07:08:31,866 --> 07:08:35,666
all of these extensions that I'm talking about, they are registered extensions.

4134
07:08:36,126 --> 07:08:39,866
GBIF has a nice page with all these registered extensions on it.

4135
07:08:40,446 --> 07:08:43,446
And we found that the extended measurement or fact, it

4136
07:08:43,706 --> 07:08:47,426
incorporated or was able to incorporate phenological data just fine because we

4137
07:08:47,486 --> 07:08:51,366
could link it to measurement types and measurement values and

4138
07:08:51,626 --> 07:08:54,722
who did it How they did it, and whether there were any notes.

4139
07:08:54,902 --> 07:08:58,862
And then these ID values, which are found in the extended measurement or

4140
07:08:58,922 --> 07:09:01,362
fact extension, could be linked to ontologies.

4141
07:09:01,442 --> 07:09:05,222
And so we found that the phenological data could be really well captured by the

4142
07:09:05,242 --> 07:09:08,842
extended measurement or fact, as long as we had some great

4143
07:09:09,182 --> 07:09:13,062
ontologies and controlled vocabularies that we could store in these ID fields.

4144
07:09:14,242 --> 07:09:16,862
Well, they're going in the type and the value, but they are linked to the

4145
07:09:16,922 --> 07:09:18,502
ontologies through the ID fields.

4146
07:09:21,142 --> 07:09:24,802
And some implementation of this is that project was able to capture lots of

4147
07:09:24,841 --> 07:09:28,722
phenological data for the herbarium specimens in that portal using

4148
07:09:28,782 --> 07:09:31,922
some new tools that were developed, some trait scoring tools in the portal.

4149
07:09:32,342 --> 07:09:35,782
And then now that data is available for public reuse for research.

4150
07:09:36,722 --> 07:09:40,422
We were able to do some analyses of phenological trends

4151
07:09:40,542 --> 07:09:42,542
using all that data in that extension.

4152
07:09:42,602 --> 07:09:45,882
So it was pretty easy to use and incorporate and analyze from there.

4153
07:09:47,862 --> 07:09:51,802
Okay. Symbiota also has the material sample extension, and this has

4154
07:09:51,842 --> 07:09:55,742
largely been used by the NEON, the National Ecological Observatory Network in the

4155
07:09:55,802 --> 07:09:59,522
United States, and they needed to track the DNA extractions

4156
07:09:59,582 --> 07:10:02,162
largely and the sample extractions from their specimens.

4157
07:10:02,762 --> 07:10:05,762
And so this has been enabled in the NEON portal.

4158
07:10:06,242 --> 07:10:08,362
But there's also many other portals that it's available in too.

4159
07:10:08,382 --> 07:10:12,302
And so that's like an up-and-coming extension that folks are using

4160
07:10:12,342 --> 07:10:16,322
to track the genetic subsamples and sometimes just tissue

4161
07:10:16,402 --> 07:10:17,382
subsamples of

4162
07:10:19,122 --> 07:10:22,082
the specimens. And so this one actually, I think it has a lot of potential to

4163
07:10:22,102 --> 07:10:25,302
continue to be used for subsampling of specimens.

4164
07:10:26,482 --> 07:10:29,622
Here is just a screenshot of what the material sample module looks like, and

4165
07:10:31,282 --> 07:10:34,882
most of them are elements of the GGBN material sample extension.

4166
07:10:39,542 --> 07:10:43,422
We also have the support for the alternative identifiers extension.

4167
07:10:43,462 --> 07:10:46,842
And the need for this came about because we found that folks were using the other

4168
07:10:46,902 --> 07:10:49,382
catalog numbers field, which previously was just a field,

4169
07:10:50,302 --> 07:10:52,142
to store a lot of different identifiers.

4170
07:10:52,202 --> 07:10:56,102
For example, national forest numbers or national parks numbers they

4171
07:10:56,122 --> 07:10:59,362
were capturing in there. But they were really difficult to search because if you're

4172
07:10:59,382 --> 07:11:00,862
just searching a text field

4173
07:11:01,802 --> 07:11:04,702
that has a lot of concatenated values in it, it's very hard to find.

4174
07:11:05,162 --> 07:11:06,282
So we ended up

4175
07:11:07,642 --> 07:11:10,342
turning this into an identifier value somewhat table.

4176
07:11:10,402 --> 07:11:11,982
So it's a different table in the structure now.

4177
07:11:12,022 --> 07:11:14,642
And it's actually really well-aligned with an existing

4178
07:11:15,782 --> 07:11:19,262
Darwin Core extension, the alternative identifiers extension, which is

4179
07:11:19,662 --> 07:11:23,602
super easy. It's basically just three or four fields saying,

4180
07:11:23,642 --> 07:11:24,922
"Here is the

4181
07:11:25,782 --> 07:11:29,602
core ID, and then the additional identifier and the additional identifier name."

4182
07:11:29,782 --> 07:11:33,582
So while this is ballooning up the number of extensions, it makes it way

4183
07:11:33,662 --> 07:11:36,842
easier to find those values when you're searching for them.

4184
07:11:38,842 --> 07:11:41,782
Okay. Lastly, I'm going to talk about the resource relationship extension that

4185
07:11:41,802 --> 07:11:45,142
we've been able to use in Symbiota portals, and this has been really key for

4186
07:11:45,202 --> 07:11:49,182
capturing extended specimen data, and so we're really excited to see where this one

4187
07:11:49,242 --> 07:11:53,162
goes. So we are using the Darwin Core resource relationship extension,

4188
07:11:53,282 --> 07:11:57,242
which is very confusing to look at because all of the names sound similar,

4189
07:11:57,982 --> 07:12:01,802
in English at least. And so, it does take quite a bit

4190
07:12:01,922 --> 07:12:03,462
of time to wrap your mind around it.

4191
07:12:03,802 --> 07:12:07,322
But the idea is, is that you're providing an identifier to a thing,

4192
07:12:07,782 --> 07:12:11,442
a thing it's related to, and the relationship of that thing to the thing that it's

4193
07:12:11,482 --> 07:12:14,002
related to. And then, of course, there's IDs for all those things.

4194
07:12:14,842 --> 07:12:17,422
So we've seen some really cool implementation for this, is

4195
07:12:18,362 --> 07:12:22,282
you are able to make relationships from a specimen record to

4196
07:12:22,362 --> 07:12:25,582
something else. And that can be either another specimen record or completely

4197
07:12:25,642 --> 07:12:26,142
external,

4198
07:12:27,582 --> 07:12:27,662
or a

4199
07:12:28,802 --> 07:12:32,762
specimen record some other place, or a completely different thing,

4200
07:12:32,902 --> 07:12:36,402
so a different site or a different reference or something like that.

4201
07:12:36,902 --> 07:12:40,302
So one implementation of this has been the Carlquist project,

4202
07:12:41,102 --> 07:12:41,522
where

4203
07:12:42,382 --> 07:12:46,362
a library and an herbarium, actually two herbaria, are coming together and

4204
07:12:46,422 --> 07:12:50,202
trying to link all this field data, these slides data, this research and field

4205
07:12:51,042 --> 07:12:54,742
notebook data to the specimen records that were already in Symbiota portals.

4206
07:12:55,542 --> 07:12:59,182
So this is a close-up photo that was taken from the same event

