Consider a user who, God forbid, wants to search on GBIF for

5204
08:46:39,568 --> 08:46:40,888
records in the Atlantic Ocean.

5205
08:46:42,088 --> 08:46:45,696
The Atlantic Ocean is a real place But where do you put that in Darwin Core?

5206
08:46:46,756 --> 08:46:50,396
Data providers aren't mobilizing Atlantic Ocean consistently.

5207
08:46:50,736 --> 08:46:54,416
Some put it in water body or Darwin Core higher geography, some in

5208
08:46:54,456 --> 08:46:57,036
both. Maybe it's only in specific locality for some.

5209
08:46:58,696 --> 08:47:02,396
And then at the implementation level, GBIF indexes Darwin Core higher

5210
08:47:02,436 --> 08:47:06,056
geography and Darwin Core water body as text strings, which really they are,

5211
08:47:06,756 --> 08:47:09,616
rather than as concepts, which really the Atlantic Ocean is.

5212
08:47:10,216 --> 08:47:14,096
So a user won't even get all Atlantic Ocean records if the text

5213
08:47:14,136 --> 08:47:16,355
they query is Atlantic or vice versa.

5214
08:47:17,416 --> 08:47:21,376
Long story short, if you needed to aggregate records from the Atlantic Ocean, you

5215
08:47:21,456 --> 08:47:25,256
currently would need to do at least four searches or more on GBIF and compile the

5216
08:47:25,296 --> 08:47:25,776
results.

5217
08:47:28,556 --> 08:47:32,356
Another complication for data providers mobilizing geographic data is

5218
08:47:32,416 --> 08:47:36,056
that geography is fluid. Maps get redrawn, tectonic

5219
08:47:36,096 --> 08:47:39,896
plates move. That means geographic concepts are most useful when

5220
08:47:39,956 --> 08:47:40,956
associated with time.

5221
08:47:42,696 --> 08:47:46,456
For a relatively recent example, Valencia County in New Mexico lost

5222
08:47:46,496 --> 08:47:50,316
almost 81% of its territory in 1981 upon the

5223
08:47:50,356 --> 08:47:54,096
creation of Cibola County, which occupies the westernmost portion of the former

5224
08:47:54,476 --> 08:47:55,996
Valencia County's former area.

5225
08:47:57,056 --> 08:48:00,876
So shown here is a specimen record on GBIF that illustrates how confusing such

5226
08:48:00,916 --> 08:48:03,536
changes can be to an unsuspecting user.

5227
08:48:04,996 --> 08:48:07,976
When this toad was collected, it was collected in Valencia County.

5228
08:48:08,456 --> 08:48:10,076
That's what the data from the collection says.

5229
08:48:10,636 --> 08:48:10,736
But

5230
08:48:11,856 --> 08:48:15,796
the record is georeferenced, and now those coordinates fall in Cibola County,

5231
08:48:15,876 --> 08:48:16,656
according to GADM.

5232
08:48:18,016 --> 08:48:21,956
Darwin Core doesn't tell us anything about how to decide what timestamp of higher

5233
08:48:21,996 --> 08:48:25,556
geography to use, and maybe it shouldn't. Probably it shouldn't.

5234
08:48:25,996 --> 08:48:29,836
But again, we're left in a situation where more explicit data would help.

5235
08:48:32,936 --> 08:48:36,876
Okay. So then from the data management perspective, we have a few more pain

5236
08:48:36,916 --> 08:48:37,296
points.

5237
08:48:38,356 --> 08:48:42,256
We just talked about higher geography being fluid and

5238
08:48:42,296 --> 08:48:46,096
what to do, what data providers should change or not.

5239
08:48:46,896 --> 08:48:50,396
Darwin Core treats physical geography a lot less explicitly than human

5240
08:48:50,456 --> 08:48:50,996
geography.

5241
08:48:51,936 --> 08:48:54,076
What conventions should data providers follow?

5242
08:48:55,056 --> 08:48:58,776
And then finally, the big elephant in the room is Darwin Core higher geography is

5243
08:48:58,836 --> 08:49:01,016
so flexible, what do we even put in there?

5244
08:49:04,176 --> 08:49:06,396
So what to do about all this unruliness?

